Identify the core distinction in licensing approaches between GitLab and GitHub. 
Core distinction in licensing approaches
GitLab: The core GitLab Community Edition is open-source, allowing users to view, modify, and distribute its source code under the applicable open-source license.
GitHub:GitHub is primarily a proprietary, closed-source platform owned by Microsoft, although it supports and hosts many open-source projects.
In short: GitLab has a stronger,open-source/self-hosting licensing approach, while GitHub is mainly a proprietary hosted service with open-source repositories hosted on it.Identify and list at least five features that are common to both GitHub and GitLab.

 Common Features of GitHub and GitLab
1. Git Repository Hosting – Both allow users to create, store, and manage Git repositories online.
2. Version Control – Both track changes to source code and allow users to work with different versions.
3. Issue Tracking – Both allow users to report bugs, create tasks, and track project issues.
4. Code Review – Both allow developers to review code changes before they are added to the main project.
5. Collaboration – Both allow multiple developers to work together on the same project.
6. Branching and Merging – Both allow developers to create branches and merge completed changes into the main code.
7. CI/CD – Both provide tools for automatically testing, building, and deploying software.


Two features that are unique or particularly distinctive to GitHub are:
1. GitHub Pages– Allows users to host websites directly from a GitHub repository, commonly used for project documentation, portfolios, and static websites.
2. GitHub Codespaces – Provides cloud-based development environments where developers can write, run, and test code directly in a browser without setting up the development environment locally.Identify and list at least two features that are unique to GitLab.

Two features that are unique or particularly distinctive to GitLab are:
1. GitLab Auto DevOps– Automatically provides predefined CI/CD pipelines to help build, test, secure, and deploy applications with less manual configuration.
2. Built-in Container Registry– GitLab includes an integrated registry for storing, managing, and deploying Docker/container images directly within a GitLab project.







